# NestJS Entity Module Encapsulation

Each entity/domain should be implemented as a self-contained module that owns its API and persistence logic.

## Structure per module

- Keep entity code together under one folder (example: `backend/src/users`).
- Use subfolders: `model`, `services`, `controllers`, `mappers`.
- Put all entity-specific logic in the same module; avoid leaking it to unrelated modules.

## Module wiring standard

- Register module schemas in `MongooseModule.forFeatureAsync([...])`.
- Declare all entity-related services in `providers`.
- Declare route handlers in `controllers`.
- Export only services needed by other modules.
- Use `forwardRef` only when a real circular dependency exists.

## Responsibilities

- **Model**: schema fields, indexes, plugins, persistence shape.
- **Service**: business logic + data access; extends shared base service when available.
- **Controller**: HTTP layer only (validation/guards/route orchestration), delegates to service.
- **Mapper**: explicit model-to-contract DTO mapping; controllers return mapped DTOs, not raw model docs.

## `users` module reference

- Module: `backend/src/users/users.module.ts`
- Models: `backend/src/users/model/*.ts`
- Services: `backend/src/users/services/*.ts`
- Controllers: `backend/src/users/controllers/*.ts`
- Mappers: `backend/src/users/mappers/*.ts`

## Checklist for new entity modules

- Module contains model + services + controllers + mappers.
- Controller methods call service methods and return mapped DTOs.
- Service owns query/filter/paging logic for the entity.
- Contracts are consumed from `@app/contracts` instead of redefining shapes locally.
